From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: novatek-nt36532: unbind before the host of the second link

The panel registers its device on the second DSI host itself, as a child
of that host. If the host goes first, as it does when the module of the
host driver goes away, it takes that device along. The panel driver then
detaches and unregisters a device that is gone, and KASAN sees a
use-after-free before the oops.

Make the panel a consumer of that host, so that it unbinds first. It
still comes back the same way, as the host registers a new device.

Found with a register model in QEMU, by unloading the module.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36532.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36532.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36532.c
index c01d78280cf45985e6535adf5eaf77c8626ad940..b45217dcbc61fac4eef770b8dd3f980f6d0d6afd 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36532.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36532.c
@@ -822,6 +822,13 @@ static int nt36532_probe(struct mipi_dsi_device *dsi)
 			return dev_err_probe(dev, -EPROBE_DEFER,
 					     "cannot get secondary DSI host\n");
 
+		/*
+		 * The device below is a child of that host, and this driver lets go of
+		 * it when it unbinds. So it has to unbind before that host can go.
+		 */
+		if (!device_link_add(dev, dsi1_host->dev, DL_FLAG_AUTOREMOVE_CONSUMER))
+			dev_warn(dev, "cannot link to secondary DSI host\n");
+
 		ctx->dsi[1] = devm_mipi_dsi_device_register_full(dev, dsi1_host,
 								 &dsi_info);
 		if (IS_ERR(ctx->dsi[1])) {
